In my case, when I converted a PyTorch model to OpenVINO using ov.convert_model() without using ONNX (no distillation), the inference time was sped up from 22 minutes to 17 minutes.

As a side note, the biggest speed-up trick of OpenVINO is the ability to fuse convolution and batch normalization into a single computational block, but this doesn't work with all BBs. For example, eca_nfnet_l0 achieves very high accuracy with a single model, but NFNet (Normalization-Free Network), as the name suggests, is an architecture that completely eliminates batch normalization, so it's not sped up when converted to OV. Therefore, it seems that a lot of experimentation is needed to choose the model that offers the best balance of accuracy and computational cost in BB.


